title:    Break one tunnel. BGP routes around it in three seconds.
subtitle: measure-failover.py — three trials, routes withdrawn and restored, nobody editing a route table
width:    980
---
$ python scripts/measure-failover.py --trials 3

[12:56:23] baseline: 2 route(s) to 10.30.0.0/24, 2 BGP session(s) Established

--- trial 1 ---
[12:56:30] hub-peer-t0 disabled
[12:56:33] route withdrawn after 3.6s
[12:56:40] hub-peer-t0 re-enabled
[12:56:43] route restored after 3.1s

--- trial 2 ---
[12:56:50] hub-peer-t0 disabled
[12:56:53] route withdrawn after 3.1s
[12:57:03] route restored after 3.3s

--- trial 3 ---
[12:57:11] hub-peer-t0 disabled
[12:57:14] route withdrawn after 3.3s
[12:57:24] route restored after 3.4s

[12:57:33] restored: 2 route(s), 2 session(s) Established

RESULT: routes withdrawn in 3.1-3.6s across 3 trials.

# TWO routes to one prefix is the whole point. Each tunnel advertises
# 10.30.0.0/24 independently, so the count is the failover signal: 2 means
# both paths live, 1 means BGP has withdrawn the dead one and the survivor
# is carrying the traffic.
#
# Nothing in this lab's Terraform contains 10.30.0.0/24 as a route. It is
# declared once as a SUBNET on the branch side and arrives on the hub side
# because the two routers told each other about it.
#
# A static route cannot do this. It points at a dead tunnel exactly as
# confidently as a live one, and keeps sending traffic into a hole until a
# human notices. That is why BGP is not decoration on HA VPN - it is the
# mechanism that makes the HA real.
